<Window xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:vm="using:Parakeet.App.ViewModels"
        xmlns:views="using:Parakeet.App.Views"
        xmlns:sys="using:System"
        x:Class="Parakeet.App.Views.MainWindow"
        x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel"
        Title="Uindosill - local transcription"
        Icon="avares://Uindosill/Assets/uindosill.ico"
        Width="1080" Height="720"
        MinWidth="920" MinHeight="520"
        Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}"
        Foreground="{DynamicResource InkBrush}"
        FontFamily="{DynamicResource SansFamily}"
        ExtendClientAreaToDecorationsHint="True"
        WindowDecorations="BorderOnly">

  <!--
    The interface designed 2026-08-19, in the shape that design specifies: no OS title bar, a 46px
    headerbar carrying the application name and a pill view-switcher, and square corners on
    everything that is not a circle.

    Colours, type and control geometry are not here — they are in Theme/Tokens.axaml and
    Theme/Controls.axaml, which reach the whole application. What is here is layout and the few
    places a surface has to name its own colour because it is the only one of its kind.

    Five structural notes, because every one of them looks like an accident:

    · The pages still live in a TabControl, whose header strip is collapsed by the `headless`
      class. The design puts the switcher in the middle of the headerbar, between the application
      name and the window buttons, which is not a place a tab strip can be — so the switcher is
      one radio button per page, all bound to the same SelectedTab index the TabControl reads. The
      TabControl is doing the page-switching; the pills are the only part anyone sees. (It said
      "four radio buttons" until 2026-08-23, by which time there were five: a comment that counts
      something the file keeps growing is a comment that goes wrong quietly, so it no longer
      counts.)

    · MinWidth is measured rather than chosen, and it is the switcher that sets it. The headerbar
      is 210,*,210 with the pills centred in the star column, so the narrowest useful window is the
      pills plus both fixed columns: six of them measure 464px, and 14 (this bar's left padding)
      + 210 + 464 + 210 + 2 (the window's own edge) comes to 900. 920 is that with a little room.

      It was 820 until 2026-08-23, which fitted five pills with three pixels to spare and clipped
      the sixth: a Border does not clip its children, so the sunken rail stopped in the middle of a
      word while the last pill carried on and drew under the close button. None of that is visible
      at the width anybody screenshots at, which is why the number is pinned by a measurement —
      HeaderBarTests.TheSwitcherClearsTheNameAndTheWindowButtonsAtEveryWidthTheWindowAllows fails
      with the figure to put here if a seventh pill is ever added.

    · A control on a page that is not showing is built, named and bound — it is simply not drawn.
      The TabControl realises only the selected tab into the *visual* tree, but the whole markup
      tree is constructed at load and every Name in it is registered in the window's one name
      scope. So `this.FindControl<T>("X")` below finds the Export tab's Browse button from the
      constructor, with Transcribe showing, and its Click wiring works — measured, not assumed
      (gotcha 31). What does not find it is `GetVisualDescendants`, which walks what is drawn.

      That is why nothing in this file wires a handler in the markup: the constructor still
      reaches every page, so the file keeps one idiom. It is also the trap on the other side —
      `FindControl` answering for a control on a page nobody can reach means a test can assert a
      control exists when it is drawn nowhere, which is what gotcha 31 is actually about.

    · The corner is square by asking rather than by drawing. See Services/WindowFrame.cs — DWM
      takes a preference, not a radius, which is what makes square reachable where the earlier
      12px version of this design was not. Confirmed on Windows 11 at 250% scaling: DWM reports
      the do-not-round preference back, and the window draws square.

    · WindowDecorations="BorderOnly" removes the toolkit's own title bar, and the headerbar below
      takes its place through WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole.

      BorderOnly and NOT None, which is the trap here. None removes the whole frame along with the
      title bar: it takes WS_THICKFRAME with it, so the window stops being resizable, and it takes
      the compositor's shadow, so on a light desktop nothing shows where the application ends.
      Both were shipped that way for an afternoon and neither is visible in a screenshot of the
      window — the style bits have to be read off the handle. Without that the caption and a
      second set of window buttons are drawn over this design's own — invisible to a headless
      render, because there is no window frame there to draw, and found only by photographing the
      built application. The roles also mean the platform handles dragging and
      double-click-to-maximise, rather than a PointerPressed handler calling BeginMoveDrag.

      The cost of that arrangement: inside the TitleBar element every press is a drag unless the
      control under the pointer carries a role of its own. The window buttons have native ones
      (MinimizeButton, MaximizeButton, CloseButton); anything else interactive placed in the
      headerbar — the tab switcher — has to be marked ElementRole="User", Avalonia's carve-out
      for "this receives input even inside chrome". Without it the control renders, hovers and
      never clicks, and a headless test cannot tell: there is no platform hit-test in it.
  -->

  <Window.Resources>
    <views:IndexMatchConverter x:Key="IndexMatch" />
    <!-- Both grounds this converter draws — the search hit's, and the word being spoken — come
         from the token sheet rather than from hexes in the converter, so taro and the pastel
         yellow each keep one definition. StaticResource because a converter is a plain object
         with no styled properties for DynamicResource to track — the brushes are resolved once,
         at load, out of the application's dictionary. -->
    <views:HighlightConverter x:Key="Highlight" Hit="{StaticResource Taro200Brush}"
                              Spoken="{StaticResource SpokenWordBrush}" />
  </Window.Resources>

  <!-- The window's edge. Inside the window because there is nothing outside it to draw on: with
       WindowDecorations="None" the frame is gone, so this hairline and the compositor's shadow are
       the only things saying where the application stops. -->
  <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WindowEdgeBrush}"
          BorderThickness="1"
          Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}">
  <DockPanel>

    <!-- ══ Headerbar ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         46px, one hairline underneath, no fill. Dragging it moves the window, which is the whole
         job the OS title bar was doing before it was turned off. -->
    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
            Name="HeaderBar"
            WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="TitleBar"
            Height="46"
            Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource RuleSeenBrush}"
            BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
            Padding="14,0,0,0">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="210,*,210">

        <!-- The mark and the name. Half matcha, half taro since 2026-08-23: the product is both
             generations now, and the badge is the one surface allowed to say both at once — the
             generation rule binds surfaces that do something, and the badge only says who this
             is. The ground is a gradient with its two stops at the same offset, which is how a
             brush draws a hard seam rather than a blend; the strokes are the same glyph twice,
             the taro copy clipped to the right half, so the centre bar — which sits exactly on
             the seam — is split down its own middle. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="0" Name="Wordmark" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="9"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <Border Width="22" Height="22">
            <Border.Background>
              <LinearGradientBrush StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="100%,0%">
                <GradientStop Offset="0.5" Color="{StaticResource Matcha100}" />
                <GradientStop Offset="0.5" Color="{StaticResource Taro100}" />
              </LinearGradientBrush>
            </Border.Background>
            <Panel>
              <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" StrokeThickness="2.4"
                    StrokeLineCap="Round"
                    Data="M4 11.5v1M8.5 7v10M13 3.5v17M17.5 8.5v7M22 11.5v1"
                    Width="13" Height="13" Stretch="Uniform"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Panel Clip="M11,0 L22,0 L22,22 L11,22 Z">
                <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" StrokeThickness="2.4"
                      StrokeLineCap="Round"
                      Data="M4 11.5v1M8.5 7v10M13 3.5v17M17.5 8.5v7M22 11.5v1"
                      Width="13" Height="13" Stretch="Uniform"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </Panel>
            </Panel>
          </Border>
          <TextBlock Text="Uindosill" FontSize="13" FontWeight="Bold"
                     LetterSpacing="-0.156" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          <TextBlock Text="local transcription" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The switcher. Six pills since 2026-08-23; it opened with the line "Licences last,
             because it is the one tab nobody opens twice", which stopped being true when that tab
             retired into the About window — the reason it was last is the reason it is gone.
             Reading order and TabControl order are two lists, and both are set out below.

             ElementRole="User" because this sits inside the TitleBar element, and inside that
             element the platform answers every press as "start dragging the window" unless the
             control under the pointer says otherwise. The pills carry no native role of their own
             — the window buttons do, which is why they clicked and these did not — so they are
             marked as the interactive carve-out Avalonia provides for exactly this: input reaches
             them instead of being taken for a move. A headless render cannot show the difference;
             only a pointer on the built window can. -->
        <Border Grid.Column="1" Name="Switcher" Classes="switcher"
                WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="User"
                HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center">
          <!-- Reading order and TabControl order are two different lists, and always have been.
               What a reader sees is the work first — get words, ask them questions, get them out —
               then the two pages that configure a run, then the library and the housekeeping:

                 Transcribe · Ask · Export · Settings · Models · Updates

               What the TabControl holds is 0 Transcribe, 1 Models, 2 Export, 3 Updates, 4 Ask,
               5 Settings, and that is not an accident either. Export took index 2 because that is
               where Licences was until it retired into the About window on 2026-08-23, and taking
               the freed slot is what keeps Models at 1, Updates at 3 and Ask at 4 — the indices
               tests, settings and MainWindowViewModel.ModelsTabIndex already found them at.
               Renumbering four pages to make one list agree with the other would buy tidiness and
               spend correctness. -->
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="2">
            <RadioButton Name="TabTranscribe" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Transcribe"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}" />
            <!-- Export sits next to Transcribe because transcribing writes nothing: the files
                 come from Export's button, so the two pages are one workflow read left to
                 right — transcribe, then export. -->
            <RadioButton Name="TabExport" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Export"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabAsk" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Ask"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=4}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabSettings" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Settings"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=5}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabModels" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Models"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}" />
            <RadioButton Name="TabUpdates" Classes="segment" GroupName="Switcher" Content="Updates"
                         IsChecked="{Binding SelectedTab, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>

        <!-- Bare glyphs, no ground until hover. Spacing is 0 because each button is 40px wide
             around an 11px glyph, so the gap a reader sees is the padding rather than a margin. -->
        <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Name="WindowButtons" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="0"
                    HorizontalAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Stretch">
          <!-- Stretch="None" on all three, and the minimise glyph is why: its geometry is a
               horizontal line, so it is seven units wide and zero tall, and scaling that uniformly
               into a 9x9 box has no defined answer — Avalonia drew a dot. Left at their natural
               size the three glyphs are also consistent with each other, which uniform scaling of
               three different aspect ratios was not. -->
          <Button Name="WindowMinimise" WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MinimizeButton" Classes="window" ToolTip.Tip="Minimise">
            <Path StrokeThickness="1.3"
                  StrokeLineCap="Round" Data="M0 0h11" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
          <!-- Two overlapping squares rather than one: the restore glyph, drawn as a filled-edge
               front square with the top-right of a second showing behind it. Deliberately the same
               glyph in both window states — a button that changes shape under the pointer is a
               button people misclick, and the tooltip says which way it goes. -->
          <Button Name="WindowMaximise" WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="MaximizeButton" Classes="window" ToolTip.Tip="Maximise">
            <Path StrokeThickness="1.3"
                  StrokeJoin="Round" StrokeLineCap="Round"
                  Data="M0 3.2h7.8v7.8h-7.8z M2.9 3.2v-2.9h7.8v7.8h-2.9"
                  Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
          <Button Name="WindowClose" WindowDecorationProperties.ElementRole="CloseButton" Classes="window close" ToolTip.Tip="Close">
            <Path StrokeThickness="1.3"
                  StrokeLineCap="Round" Data="M0 0l10.5 10.5M10.5 0l-10.5 10.5" Stretch="None" />
          </Button>
        </StackPanel>
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <!-- ══ Update notice ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         The visible half of the update decision: above the pages rather than inside one, because a
         notice on a tab nobody opens is not a notice, and hidden entirely when there is nothing to
         say, so the normal window is exactly as it was.

         Matcha rather than the blue it used to be. Blue belongs to no generation in this system,
         and an update is a v1 thing. -->
    <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top"
            Name="UpdateNotice"
            IsVisible="{Binding Updates.IsUpdateAvailable}"
            Background="{DynamicResource Matcha50Brush}"
            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Matcha200Brush}"
            BorderThickness="0,0,0,1"
            Padding="16,10">
      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Updates.Notice}"
                   Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
        <Button Grid.Column="1" Classes="primary" Content="Download and restart"
                Command="{Binding Updates.InstallCommand}"
                IsEnabled="{Binding Updates.CanInstall}" Margin="12,0,0,0" />
      </Grid>
    </Border>

    <TabControl Name="Tabs" Classes="headless" SelectedIndex="{Binding SelectedTab}">

      <!-- ══ Transcribe ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <TabItem Header="Transcribe">
        <!-- Three rows since 2026-08-23. The fourth was the 30-second segmentation footnote, which
             went to the Settings tab with the segment cap it explains: an explanation of a control
             on another page is a paragraph nobody can act on. -->
        <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,*" Margin="16" DataContext="{Binding Transcribe}"
              x:DataType="vm:TranscribeViewModel">

          <!-- Shut while a batch runs, because the queue behind it is. A zone that keeps taking
               drops the view model then discards is a gesture accepted and thrown away, and the
               row it used to leave behind sat blank at "Waiting" for ever. Open it is matcha-50
               inside a dashed matcha-400 edge; shut it is the sunken grey with no dash. -->
          <Border Grid.Row="0"
                  Name="DropZone"
                  DragDrop.AllowDrop="{Binding !IsRunning}"
                  Background="{DynamicResource Matcha50Brush}"
                  Margin="0,0,0,12">
            <!-- The dash is a Rectangle rather than the Border's own edge: in Avalonia 12.1.1
                 StrokeDashArray is a Shape property and Border has no dashed variant, so a border
                 here could only ever be solid. Drawn under the content in the same cell, so it
                 does not take part in the layout. -->
            <Panel>
              <Rectangle Stroke="{DynamicResource Matcha400Brush}" StrokeThickness="1"
                         StrokeDashArray="4,3" />
              <StackPanel Spacing="6" HorizontalAlignment="Center" Margin="18">
                <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" StrokeThickness="1.9"
                      StrokeLineCap="Round" StrokeJoin="Round"
                      Data="M12 3v11m0 0l-4-4m4 4l4-4M4 17v2a2 2 0 002 2h12a2 2 0 002-2v-2"
                      Width="19" Height="19" Stretch="Uniform" HorizontalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding DropHint}" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           FontSize="14.5" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SupportedExtensionsHint}" HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                           Classes="mono meta" FontSize="10.5"
                           IsVisible="{Binding !IsRunning}" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Panel>
          </Border>

          <!-- ── Or paste a link ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
               Beside dropping a file rather than instead of it, and deliberately the same row
               height and the same tinted family: a link is another way to get a recording into the
               queue, not a different feature. The audio track is what is downloaded — the
               transcript is made from sound — and the Ask tab streams the picture from the link
               when it wants one, so nothing here keeps a copy of a video.

               Shut while a batch runs, for the same reason the drop zone above is. -->
          <Border Grid.Row="1" Name="LinkRow" Classes="tinted" Padding="11,9" Margin="0,0,0,12"
                  VerticalAlignment="Bottom">
            <StackPanel Spacing="7">
              <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Or a link" Classes="secondary"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,0,10,0" />
                <TextBox Grid.Column="1" Name="LinkBox" Text="{Binding Url}"
                         IsEnabled="{Binding CanAddUrl}"
                         PlaceholderText="Paste a video or podcast link: the audio is downloaded and transcribed" />
                <Button Grid.Column="2" Name="FetchLink" Classes="primary" Content="Fetch"
                        Margin="8,0,0,0"
                        Command="{Binding FetchUrlCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanFetchUrl}" />
              </Grid>

              <!-- What the fetch is doing, or why it failed. One line, and absent when there is
                   nothing to say rather than a permanent empty row. -->
              <TextBlock Name="LinkStatus" Text="{Binding UrlStatus}" Classes="fine"
                         Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding UrlStatus, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <!-- Why the box is dead, when it is: this build vendored no downloader. -->
              <TextBlock Name="LinkHint" Text="{Binding UrlHint}" Classes="fine warning"
                         IsVisible="{Binding UrlHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>

          <Grid Grid.Row="2" ColumnDefinitions="344,14,*">

            <!-- ── The queue ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Column="0">
              <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7" Margin="0,10,0,0">
                <Button Classes="primary" Content="Start" Command="{Binding StartCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanStart}" />
                <!-- Start runs what has not been run, so redoing a finished queue is asked for
                     rather than guessed at: this is the only way to transcribe the same files a
                     second time, and it is live only when there is a finished row to redo. -->
                <Button Content="Run again" Command="{Binding RunAgainCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding CanRunAgain}" />
                <Button Content="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding IsRunning}" />
                <Button Content="Clear" Command="{Binding ClearCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding !IsRunning}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- Start is disabled until a model is loaded, and again once every file in the
                   queue is transcribed, so it has to say which. -->
              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Text="{Binding StartHint}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Classes="fine warning" Margin="0,8,0,0"
                         IsVisible="{Binding StartHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding Jobs}" SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedJob}">
                <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:JobViewModel">
                    <StackPanel Spacing="5">
                      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto">
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding DisplayName}" Classes="emphasis"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding ProgressLabel}" Classes="meta"
                                   Margin="7,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding ProgressLabel, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                      </Grid>
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="meta" />
                      <!-- Gone once the row is finished. A full bar under "Done — 6 files" is a
                           progress report for something that is not in progress, and it made every
                           completed row look like a fifth thing still running. Queued rows keep an
                           empty one, because they are waiting rather than done. -->
                      <ProgressBar Value="{Binding Progress}" Maximum="100"
                                   IsIndeterminate="{Binding IsIndeterminate}"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding !IsFinished}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Error}" Classes="fine error"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding Error, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding Warning}" Classes="fine warning"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding Warning, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                      <!-- Which model and backend produced the speaker labels. The window named the
                           backend the transcription ran on and never the one the labels came from,
                           so a GPU diarisation and a CPU one were indistinguishable here — see
                           JobViewModel.SpeakerProvenance for why this is the ordinary voice rather
                           than the warning one. -->
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeakerProvenance}" Classes="fine meta"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerProvenance, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                      <!-- And the same for the English, which was as silent as the labels were:
                           DescribeTranslator speaks only when parity has a finding or auto fell
                           back. Two lines rather than one, because the two passes are independent
                           — either runs without the other, and either fails while the other does
                           not. -->
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding TranslationProvenance}" Classes="fine meta"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding TranslationProvenance, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
              </ListBox>
            </DockPanel>

            <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1" Background="Transparent" />

            <!-- ── The transcript ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Column="2">
              <!-- ── The two extra passes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                   Beside the queue rather than on Settings with the cut controls, since later on
                   2026-08-23: these two decide what a run produces for the queue on this page,
                   and a per-run decision belongs on the page where the run is launched. Both are
                   off by default and both cost time, so neither happens unless it is asked for
                   here. A run writes no files — the Export tab's button does, from the format
                   ticks beside it — so the passes are the whole of what Start needs asked.

                   Translation first and speakers last, so the strip that grows when ticked — the
                   count field and up to two sentences appear under the speaker box — moves only
                   the transcript below it and never the other opt-in. -->
              <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top" Spacing="7" Margin="0,0,0,11">

                <!-- The English opt-in, drawn as the twin of the speaker one below: its own
                     tinted strip, off by default, disabled with a reason rather than hidden while
                     its model is not installed.

                     Matcha, because translating is a v1 feature. There is no language picker
                     beside it and that is a finding rather than an omission — the checkpoint is
                     many-to-one, told the target and never the source, so there is nothing for a
                     picker to say. The direction is fixed: into English, never out of it. -->
                <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="10,8">
                  <DockPanel LastChildFill="True">
                    <CheckBox DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                              Name="TranslateToEnglish"
                              Content="Translate to English"
                              FontWeight="SemiBold"
                              Margin="0,0,9,0"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              IsChecked="{Binding TranslateToEnglish}"
                              IsEnabled="{Binding CanTranslate}" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding TranslationHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               IsVisible="{Binding TranslationHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                  </DockPanel>
                </Border>

                <!-- The speaker opt-in. Off by default; disabled with a reason, not hidden, while
                     no labeller is in the build — a control that silently does nothing is the
                     failure this application refuses to ship.

                     Matcha, because labelling speakers is a v1 feature. It sits on its own tinted
                     strip so the opt-in reads as a decision rather than another checkbox. -->
                <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="10,8">
                  <!-- The outer panel stacks vertically, which is what makes the wrapping below
                       work: a StackPanel measures its children with infinite size along the axis
                       it stacks on, so only the horizontal ones break TextWrapping. -->
                  <StackPanel Spacing="7">
                    <!-- DockPanel, not a horizontal StackPanel: a StackPanel measures its children
                         with infinite width on the stacking axis, so TextWrapping on the hint
                         never engaged and the reason the opt-in was disabled ran off the right
                         edge — "…it is a 453 MiB dow". The hint is the whole point of disabling
                         rather than hiding the control, so it has to be readable. -->
                    <DockPanel LastChildFill="True">
                      <CheckBox DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                                Name="LabelSpeakers"
                                Content="Label speakers"
                                FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                IsChecked="{Binding LabelSpeakers}"
                                IsEnabled="{Binding CanLabelSpeakers}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeakerHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                    </DockPanel>

                    <!-- The speaker count, and the only repair this product has for a recording
                         longer than the diariser's labels are established on. Hidden rather than
                         disabled while the opt-in is off, which is the one place this window
                         departs from its own convention and does so deliberately: a disabled
                         control is shown when the user cannot reach the thing it needs, and here
                         they can — the checkbox is right above it and ticking it brings this to
                         life. It is a detail of the opt-in, not a setting of its own.

                         Blank runs, and there is no default of two, because the count has to come
                         from the user for the fold to mean anything. The fold this drives is wrong
                         to apply unasked: on one of the eighteen AMI development meetings the two
                         genuinely different speakers who collide least never overlap at all, so a
                         guessed default merges two real people there.

                         **Blank was refused until 2026-08-28, and that refusal was Sortformer's.**
                         It was argued from a fifty-minute bound past which that model's estimate
                         drifted — a figure this project measured on Sortformer, which left with it
                         for attic/sortformer/. The model here declares no bound and no cap, so the
                         refusal was resting on evidence that no longer exists while the sentence
                         under the field said, in one breath, that the count was required and that
                         the model works it out for itself. Requiring it also made the destructive
                         reading the default: the number never reaches the clustering either way, so
                         a count below the truth does not correct an over-split, it merges two real
                         people. The command line has always taken this as an optional flag; the two
                         now agree. -->
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8"
                                IsVisible="{Binding CanSetSpeakerCount}">
                      <TextBlock Text="How many speakers" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <!-- Wide enough for the placeholder, which is the longest thing this field
                           ever shows. At 128 the spin buttons ate it and it read "estimat", which
                           a headless render would not have caught and the opened window did. -->
                      <NumericUpDown Value="{Binding SpeakerCount}" Minimum="1" Maximum="99"
                                     Increment="1" Width="170" FormatString="0"
                                     PlaceholderText="optional" />
                    </StackPanel>

                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeakerCountHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerCountHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                    <!-- Past where the evidence stops, said before the batch rather than beside
                         the finished transcript, on the same screen as the queue and the Start
                         button it is a warning about. The command line has fired this since the
                         bound was measured. -->
                    <!-- No MaxWidth: it wraps to the strip like the hint above it, and a paragraph
                         that broke at a different measure from the one directly over it reads as a
                         different column rather than as the next thing to know. -->
                    <TextBlock Name="DurationWarning"
                               Text="{Binding SpeakerDurationWarning}" Classes="warning"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               IsVisible="{Binding SpeakerDurationWarning, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </Border>
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Classes="meta" FontSize="11.5" Margin="0,10,0,0" />

              <!-- The transcript/English switcher, and it exists only when there is something to
                   switch to: a run that did not translate gets no switcher rather than a second
                   pill that leads to an empty pane.

                   The same pills as the headerbar's view switcher, which is one of the four shapes
                   in this design that keeps a corner — a pill reads as tappable. Two panes rather
                   than one pane with a toggle, because the point is to compare: the English is
                   produced *beside* the transcript, not instead of it, and the times, the speakers
                   and their chips are the same on both sides.

                   Left-aligned rather than centred: the headerbar's switcher is the window's, and
                   a second centred pill row underneath it would read as a peer of that rather than
                   as a control belonging to the panel below. -->
              <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="switcher" Name="TranscriptPaneSwitcher"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,0,0,9"
                      IsVisible="{Binding CanShowTranslation}">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                  <RadioButton Name="PaneTranscript" Classes="segment" GroupName="TranscriptPane"
                               Content="Transcript"
                               IsChecked="{Binding TranscriptPane, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}" />
                  <RadioButton Name="PaneEnglish" Classes="segment" GroupName="TranscriptPane"
                               Content="English"
                               IsChecked="{Binding TranscriptPane, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

              <!-- The transcript of the selected file: selectable, read-only, one row per segment
                   with the speaker as a chip rather than a "S1: " prefix. The chip is what makes a
                   labelled transcript readable at a glance, and it is a v1 feature, so it is
                   matcha — see the four chip styles in Theme/Controls.axaml. Unlabelled
                   transcripts simply have no chip and read as paragraphs.

                   Bound to VisibleLines rather than to SelectedJob.Lines, because which of the two
                   collections that is depends on the switcher above. -->
              <Border Classes="panel" Padding="15,13">
                <!-- Named for the code-behind: while a batch runs, new lines keep the view at the
                     end so the newest words are the visible ones — unless the reader has scrolled
                     up, in which case their position is theirs. See FollowTranscript. -->
                <ScrollViewer Name="TranscriptScroll">
                  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding VisibleLines}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:TranscriptLineViewModel">
                        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" Margin="0,0,0,9">
                          <Border Grid.Column="0" Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                  IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeaker}"
                                  Classes="speaker"
                                  Classes.chip0="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}"
                                  Classes.chip1="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}"
                                  Classes.chip2="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}"
                                  Classes.chip3="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}"
                                  Classes.chip4="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=4}"
                                  Classes.chip5="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=5}"
                                  Classes.chip6="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=6}"
                                  Classes.chip7="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=7}">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Voice.Name}" />
                          </Border>
                          <SelectableTextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Text}"
                                               TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="20.3" />
                        </Grid>
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>
                </ScrollViewer>
              </Border>
            </DockPanel>
          </Grid>
        </Grid>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Models ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <TabItem Header="Models">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="344,14,*" Margin="16" DataContext="{Binding Models}"
              x:DataType="vm:ModelsViewModel">

          <DockPanel Grid.Column="0">
            <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="{Binding ModelDirectory}" Classes="mono meta"
                       FontSize="10.5" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,0,0,9" />
            <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding Models}" SelectedItem="{Binding Selected}">
              <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:ModelViewModel">
                  <StackPanel Spacing="5">
                    <!-- Wrapping, not a horizontal row: the diariser's name plus its badge is
                         wider than the 344px list, and in a StackPanel the badge simply ran off
                         the edge — "SPEAKERS" arrived as "SPE". -->
                    <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="6" LineSpacing="4">
                      <!-- MaxWidth so the longest name in the catalogue wraps rather than running
                           off the edge: a WrapPanel hands a child its desired width, and a single
                           line of text desires all of it. The translation entry is the one that
                           needs it. -->
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding DisplayName}" Classes="emphasis"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="316"
                                 VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                      <!-- Which model is in memory, marked in the list itself. "Installed" is true
                           of every downloaded entry and answers a different question. -->
                      <Border Classes="badge solid" IsVisible="{Binding IsLoaded}">
                        <TextBlock Text="LOADED" />
                      </Border>
                      <!-- A model that names speakers rather than writing words: downloadable
                           here, never offered to Load as the engine.

                           Outlined matcha, not the solid purple it was. Speaker labelling is a v1
                           feature and #6B4E9C sits in taro's neighbourhood, so it would have read
                           as a v2 feature the moment an Ask tab exists. The outline is what still
                           separates it from the solid LOADED badge beside it. -->
                      <Border Classes="badge outlined" IsVisible="{Binding !IsTranscriptionModel}">
                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding TaskLabel}" />
                      </Border>
                    </WrapPanel>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="meta" />
                    <ProgressBar Value="{Binding Progress}" Maximum="100"
                                 IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ListBox>
          </DockPanel>

          <GridSplitter Grid.Column="1" Background="Transparent" />

          <ScrollViewer Grid.Column="2">
           <!-- Two blocks, and the outer panel is what lets the second one exist. Everything about
                a catalogue entry is hidden when no entry is highlighted, which is right; the
                sideloaded section below is about the folder rather than about an entry, and
                nesting it inside that visibility would have hidden the one thing on this tab that
                reports what the catalogue cannot describe. -->
           <StackPanel Spacing="12">
            <StackPanel Spacing="12" IsVisible="{Binding Selected, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">

              <StackPanel Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.DisplayName}" Classes="title" />
                <!-- The upstream artefact's own name, in the monospace this design reserves for
                     text you copy rather than read. It is here so that renaming the entry to
                     something a user understands does not cost anyone the ability to check which
                     exact weights are installed against a model card or a run report. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.TechnicalName}" Classes="mono meta"
                           FontSize="10.5" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="0,1,0,3" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Provenance}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Name="Provenance"
                           FontSize="11.5"
                           Classes="provenance"
                           Classes.verified="{Binding Selected.ProvenanceIsVerified}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Notes}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" />

              <!-- Above the download button and below the description, because it is about whether
                   to press it. A warning and not a refusal: the button stays live. -->
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.FitWarning}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding Selected.HasFitWarning}"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Classes="secondary"
                         Foreground="{DynamicResource StatusWarningBrush}" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="24">
                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                  <TextBlock Text="LICENCE" Classes="section" Foreground="{DynamicResource InkMetaBrush}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Licence}" />
                </StackPanel>
                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                  <TextBlock Text="LANGUAGES" Classes="section" Foreground="{DynamicResource InkMetaBrush}" />
                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Languages}" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="420" />
                </StackPanel>
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- The provenance notice above tells the reader that downloading an unverified entry
                   needs an explicit opt-in. These are the controls it refers to; without them the
                   notice was pointing at nothing and the tab could not manage anything at all. -->
              <CheckBox Name="UnverifiedOptIn"
                        Content="Download this file even though Uindosill cannot check it"
                        IsChecked="{Binding AllowUnverified}"
                        IsVisible="{Binding Selected.NeedsUnverifiedOptIn}" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Download" Command="{Binding DownloadCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.CanDownload}" />
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Cancel" Command="{Binding CancelDownloadCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.IsBusy}" />
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Remove" Command="{Binding RemoveCommand}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.IsInstalled}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- ── Which model labels speakers ────────────────────────────────────────
                   Speaker labelling was the one job with two models to choose between, and neither
                   was simply better: one was included, quick and told apart at most four voices,
                   the other had no such limit but was a download. The four-voice one was retired to
                   attic/sortformer/ on 2026-08-27, so the choice has one option and this control is
                   effectively never enabled — kept because a second entry is the obvious next thing
                   to want and would need it back. Only shown on a speaker model, and only when
                   there is something to say. -->
              <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsVisible="{Binding Selected.IsDiarisationModel}">
                <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Use this one for speakers"
                        Command="{Binding UseForSpeakersCommand}"
                        CommandParameter="{Binding Selected}"
                        IsEnabled="{Binding Selected.CanUseForSpeakers}" />
                <TextBlock Text="Speaker labelling uses this model."
                           Classes="meta"
                           IsVisible="{Binding Selected.IsActiveDiariser}" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- Where an entry that is not the recogniser actually runs. This was a
                   sentence inside the engine panel, which only needed saying because the
                   panel was drawn under entries it had nothing to do with. It belongs to
                   the entry, so it sits with the rest of what the entry says about itself. -->
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding WhereItRuns}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         IsVisible="{Binding HasWhereItRuns}" />

              <ProgressBar Value="{Binding Selected.Progress}" Maximum="100" Height="6"
                           IsVisible="{Binding Selected.IsBusy}" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding Selected.Status}" Classes="meta" />
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding StatusMessage}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" />

            </StackPanel>

            <!-- ── The loaded model ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                 Outside the per-entry block above, and that placement is the point rather than a
                 tidy-up. There is one ModelSession per window, so this panel is global — but it
                 was drawn inside the detail pane, which put it under whichever row was highlighted
                 and made it read as a property of that row. Selecting Speaker labelling therefore
                 showed a Backend picker and a Load button beneath a model that can never be loaded
                 here and whose own provider is chosen inside the sidecar, which is exactly the
                 misreading it produced.

                 The backend row is bound here too: MainWindowViewModel has exposed Backends and
                 Backend all along and this view referenced neither, so the app was pinned to its
                 default with no way to see or change it. -->
            <!-- Drawn only on a transcription entry since 2026-08-29. It is the window's one
                 ASR engine, and under any other entry it was a block of controls about
                 something the reader had not clicked. -->
            <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="14" IsVisible="{Binding ShowEnginePanel}">
              <StackPanel Spacing="11">
                <TextBlock Text="SPEECH RECOGNITION ENGINE" Classes="section"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding LoadedSummary}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                  <TextBlock Text="Backend" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding $parent[Window].((vm:MainWindowViewModel)DataContext).Backends}"
                            SelectedItem="{Binding $parent[Window].((vm:MainWindowViewModel)DataContext).Backend}"
                            IsEnabled="{Binding !IsLoaded}" MinWidth="132" />
                  <Button Content="Load" Command="{Binding LoadCommand}"
                          IsEnabled="{Binding CanLoad}" />
                  <Button Content="Unload" Command="{Binding UnloadCommand}"
                          IsEnabled="{Binding CanUnload}" />
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- Why Load is dark, when it is. Every other disabled control in this window says
                     why; these two buttons were the exception, and a diarisation entry is the case
                     that made it visible. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding LoadHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           IsVisible="{Binding LoadHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding BackendNote}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="meta" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding $parent[Window].((vm:MainWindowViewModel)DataContext).BackendExplanation}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="meta" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Border>

            <!-- ── What else is in the folder ────────────────────────────────────────────────
                 Weights no catalogue entry claims: left behind by an older version, or put here
                 by hand. Drawn only when there are some, which for most people is never.

                 It exists because this tab names the model directory at the top of itself and
                 then described only part of its contents. Four quantisations were withdrawn from
                 the catalogue on 2026-08-20 and stayed on disk, so a tab that manages models sat
                 beside gigabytes of models it would not admit to — while `uindosill models`
                 listed them under a heading of their own. There is no Load here, deliberately:
                 nothing true can be said about weights the catalogue cannot describe, so for those
                 the only thing offered is the space back. Where the catalogue *can* name the file —
                 a multi-file entry's weights in the root rather than in the entry's own directory —
                 Move into place comes first, because there the honest options were never
                 delete-or-nothing: the entry above was offering to download what was already on
                 the disk. -->
            <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="14" IsVisible="{Binding HasSideloaded}">
              <StackPanel Spacing="11">
                <TextBlock Text="ALSO IN THIS FOLDER" Classes="section"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SideloadedSummary}" TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="meta" />

                <ListBox Name="SideloadedFiles" MaxHeight="180"
                         ItemsSource="{Binding Sideloaded}"
                         SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedSideloaded}">
                  <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                    <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:SideloadedItemViewModel">
                      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto">
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Name}" Classes="mono"
                                   FontSize="11" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding SizeLabel}" Classes="meta"
                                   Margin="10,0,0,0" />
                        <!-- Said on the row rather than only in the paragraph above, because the
                             paragraph describes a set and the Delete button acts on one line. A
                             file the catalogue names by name and size is not a stray, and the row
                             that is about to be deleted is where that has to be legible. -->
                        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                                   Text="{Binding PlacementLabel}" Classes="meta" FontSize="10.5"
                                   TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                                   IsVisible="{Binding HasPlacementLabel}" />
                      </Grid>
                    </DataTemplate>
                  </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                </ListBox>

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7">
                  <!-- First, and enabled only for weights an entry above declares: it is the
                       non-destructive way out of the state, and it costs nothing to undo by hand.
                       Delete keeps its place beside it for everything the catalogue cannot name. -->
                  <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Move into place"
                          Command="{Binding MoveIntoPlaceCommand}"
                          IsEnabled="{Binding CanMoveIntoPlace}" />
                  <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Delete file"
                          Command="{Binding RemoveSideloadedCommand}"
                          IsEnabled="{Binding CanRemoveSideloaded}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </StackPanel>
            </Border>

            <!-- The one amber panel in the window, and it earns the colour: an uninstall that
                 silently kept gigabytes of weights would be a surprise either way round. Global
                 like the two panels above it — it is about the folder, not about an entry. -->
            <Border Background="{DynamicResource WarningPanelBrush}"
                    BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WarningPanelEdgeBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="1" Padding="12,10">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="9">
                <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource StatusWarningBrush}" StrokeThickness="1.5"
                      StrokeLineCap="Round" StrokeJoin="Round"
                      Data="M8 2.9l5.6 10.2H2.4z M8 6.6v3.1 M8 11.5v.1"
                      Width="15" Height="15" Stretch="Uniform" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                <!-- The size is read off the folder rather than written here. It used to say
                     "the three of them come to over 3 GiB", which was a count of catalogue
                     entries and a total that were both true on the day they were typed and
                     neither of which is a fact about the reader's disk. -->
                <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="warning" FontSize="11.5" MaxWidth="520"
                           Text="{Binding UninstallNotice}" />
              </StackPanel>
            </Border>
           </StackPanel>
          </ScrollViewer>
        </Grid>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Export ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           The one place files come from, since later on 2026-08-23: a run fills the transcript
           on screen and writes nothing, and this page's Export button writes the ticked formats
           for the recording selected in the queue — which is why the ticks, the folder, the
           button and the way into the recording are all one page. Index 2 in the TabControl,
           which is where Licences was until it retired into the About window the same day —
           taking the freed slot is what keeps Models, Updates and Ask at the indices everything
           already found them at.

           Drawn as a reading page rather than a workbench one: one column, 26,22 margins, a title
           at the top, no splitter. Everything on it is a form field, and the three-column shape
           the Transcribe, Models and Ask tabs use is for surfaces you work inside. -->
      <TabItem Header="Export">
        <ScrollViewer Margin="26,22" DataContext="{Binding Transcribe}"
                      x:DataType="vm:TranscribeViewModel">
          <StackPanel Spacing="15" MaxWidth="700" HorizontalAlignment="Left">

            <TextBlock Text="Export" Classes="title" />
            <!-- HorizontalAlignment matters here: a stretched TextBlock with a MaxWidth centres its
                 own box in the leftover space, so the paragraph sits in the middle of the page
                 under a left-aligned heading. -->
            <TextBlock Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" LineHeight="20"
                       Text="Transcribing writes no files by itself. Tick the formats, choose where they go, and press Export to write them for the recording selected in the queue." />

            <StackPanel Spacing="9">
              <TextBlock Text="OUTPUT FORMATS" Classes="section" />
              <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Formats}">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <WrapPanel />
                  </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:OutputFormatViewModel">
                    <CheckBox Content="{Binding DisplayName}" IsChecked="{Binding IsSelected}"
                              Margin="0,0,14,0" />
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>
            </StackPanel>

            <StackPanel Spacing="9">
              <TextBlock Text="WHERE THEY GO" Classes="section" />
              <!-- Blank means beside the original, which is what the placeholder says rather than
                   leaving an empty box to be read as an error. The choice outlives the
                   application: MainWindowViewModel saves it as it changes and restores it at
                   launch while the directory still exists — a missing one, an unplugged drive,
                   goes back to blank rather than aiming every export at nowhere. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Output folder" Classes="secondary" Width="88"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBox Text="{Binding OutputDirectory}" Width="280"
                         PlaceholderText="beside each input file" />
                <!-- 30px rather than the 32 a button standing on a page takes, because this one
                     is in a row with a text box and has to match its height.

                     Its Click is still wired by name in the constructor, and moving to a tab that
                     is not the default one did not break that: the name scope holds every page's
                     controls whether or not they are drawn. See the header comment. -->
                <Button Content="Browse…" Name="BrowseOutput" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- The button the page exists for. The notice underneath is never empty while a
                   choice is missing: it says which recording the files would be for, why the
                   button is dark, or what the last press wrote — a disabled control with no
                   reason beside it is the shape of every interface defect this window has
                   shipped. -->
              <!-- ── Who is speaking ──────────────────────────────────────────
                   The same strip as the Ask tab and the same objects behind it, so a name typed on
                   either is the name on both. It is repeated rather than moved because both tabs
                   want it: one to read along with, this one to write into the files.

                   Here because this is where it changes something. ExportFilesCommand has always
                   written the named document, so the names did reach SRT, VTT, JSON and RTTM — but
                   the only place to type one was a tab about asking questions, and the button they
                   change was one tab away with nothing on this page admitting the feature existed.
                   Directly above Export, so the last thing read before pressing it is who the
                   voices are.

                   Absent on a transcript nobody labelled, which is most of them: the pass is
                   opt-in. -->
              <TextBlock Text="WHO IS SPEAKING" Classes="section" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                         IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeakers}" />

              <ItemsControl Name="ExportVoices" ItemsSource="{Binding Speakers}"
                            IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeakers}">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="7" LineSpacing="7" />
                  </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:SpeakerViewModel">
                    <Border Classes="speaker"
                            Classes.chip0="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}"
                            Classes.chip1="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}"
                            Classes.chip2="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}"
                            Classes.chip3="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}"
                            Classes.chip4="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=4}"
                            Classes.chip5="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=5}"
                            Classes.chip6="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=6}"
                            Classes.chip7="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=7}"
                            ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Label}">
                      <!-- LostFocus for the same reason as the Ask tab's: a name is redrawn when it
                           is finished, not on every keystroke. -->
                      <TextBox Text="{Binding Name, Mode=TwoWay, UpdateSourceTrigger=LostFocus}"
                               PlaceholderText="{Binding Label}"
                               MinWidth="72" />
                    </Border>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>

              <TextBlock Name="ExportRenameNotice" Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="560"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Left" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="{Binding RenameNotice}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding RenameNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <Button Name="ExportFiles" Classes="primary" Content="Export"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,4,0,0"
                      Command="{Binding ExportFilesCommand}" />

              <TextBlock Name="ExportNotice" Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="560"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Left" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="{Binding ExportNotice}" />
            </StackPanel>

            <Separator />

            <!-- ── Putting the transcript back in the recording ──────────────────────────────
                 The whole feature for somebody who does not want files: press this and the
                 recording is beside its original with the words inside it, as a subtitle track
                 that any player will show.

                 Which format goes in is read off the Output formats ticks on the Transcribe tab —
                 somebody who chose "WebVTT (words)" has already said what they want their
                 transcript to be, and asking again at the moment they press a button is asking
                 them to say it twice. The notice below names that page when no tickable format is
                 on.

                 The line underneath is not decoration. It says the name of the file that will be
                 written before it is written, says what the container costs when it costs
                 something — an MP4 cannot hold WebVTT at all, so word-by-word timing means an MKV
                 — and afterwards says what happened. A disabled button with no reason beside it is
                 the shape of every interface defect this window has shipped, so the notice is
                 there in the disabled case too, saying which of the several reasons it is. -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="9">
              <TextBlock Text="INTO THE RECORDING" Classes="section" />

              <Button Name="AddToRecording" Classes="onPage"
                      Content="Add transcript to the recording"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                      Command="{Binding AddToRecordingCommand}"
                      ToolTip.Tip="Writes a new file beside the original with the transcript inside it" />

              <TextBlock Name="AddToRecordingNotice" Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="560"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding AddToRecordingNotice}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding AddToRecordingNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
            </StackPanel>

          </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Updates ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <TabItem Header="Updates">
        <ScrollViewer Margin="26,22" DataContext="{Binding Updates}" x:DataType="vm:UpdatesViewModel">
          <StackPanel Spacing="15" MaxWidth="700" HorizontalAlignment="Left">

            <TextBlock Text="Updates" Classes="title" />

            <TextBlock Text="{Binding VersionLine}" IsVisible="{Binding HasVersionLine}"
                       FontSize="13" />

            <Border Classes="tinted" Padding="14,12">
              <SelectableTextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                   Foreground="{DynamicResource Matcha700Brush}" />
            </Border>

            <ProgressBar Minimum="0" Maximum="100" Value="{Binding Progress}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding IsBusy}" Height="6" />

            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
              <Button Classes="primary onPage" Content="Check now" Command="{Binding CheckCommand}"
                      IsEnabled="{Binding CanCheck}" />
              <Button Classes="onPage" Content="Download and restart" Name="InstallUpdate"
                      Command="{Binding InstallCommand}"
                      IsEnabled="{Binding CanInstall}" />
            </StackPanel>

            <CheckBox Content="Check for updates when Uindosill starts"
                      IsChecked="{Binding CheckOnLaunch}" />

            <Separator />

            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" FontSize="12" LineHeight="19.8"
                       Text="That check is the only thing this application does on the network without being asked. It is one HTTPS request to this project's GitHub releases page, it sends nothing about you or your files, and it downloads nothing until you press the button above. Everything else that reaches the network, downloading a model, you start yourself." />

            <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="secondary" FontSize="12" LineHeight="19.8"
                       Text="Updating replaces the application's own files. Downloaded models are kept somewhere else on purpose and are not touched by an update, or by uninstalling." />

          </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Ask ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           v2's tab, and the first surface in this application drawn in taro. Everything here is
           that colour and nothing above it is, which is the whole of the generation rule: a
           surface's colour says which product generation it belongs to.

           Two thirds of it work. The recording plays, the transcript beside it is the pipeline's
           own segments with their own times, and clicking one jumps there — none of which needs a
           language model, which is the sequencing `docs/V2-ASK-THE-TRANSCRIPT.md` asks for.

           The last third is drawn and covered. The chat panel is the shape the feature will take,
           every control in it disabled, under a notice that says it is not built. That is a
           deliberate exception to this window's own rule against shipping controls wired to
           nothing: the rule exists so that nothing here fails silently, and a panel that says in
           so many words what it cannot do is the opposite of a silent failure. What would break
           the rule is a live-looking text box that swallows a question. -->
      <TabItem Header="Ask">
        <!-- A Panel, because the queue floats now. The base layer is the page — the recording,
             its words, the chat panel — and over it sit a scrim and the recordings drawer,
             declared last so they draw on top of everything. The queue held a 228px column of
             its own until later on 2026-08-23; most of a session is spent inside one recording,
             and a resident list spent a fifth of the tab repeating what the drawer now says only
             when asked. -->
        <Panel Margin="16" DataContext="{Binding Ask}" x:DataType="vm:AskViewModel">
        <DockPanel>

          <!-- The way into the queue. A toggle rather than a button, because open and shut are
               its two states and the checked face says which one is on without a caret glyph. -->
          <ToggleButton DockPanel.Dock="Top" Name="RecordingsToggle" Classes="drawer"
                        Content="Recordings" HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,0,0,11"
                        IsChecked="{Binding IsRecordingsDrawerOpen}" />

        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,330">

          <!-- ── The recording, and its words ───────────────────────────────────
               A Grid rather than a DockPanel, because one edge in this column is draggable and a
               DockPanel has no edges: it says where a child goes by the order it was declared in,
               which is not a property a splitter can move.

               Three rows. The first is the recording — the picture with its transport flush
               underneath, the way every video player draws its controls — and the third is the
               reading. The transport shares the picture's row since later on 2026-08-23, because
               it belongs to the recording rather than to the reading: docked to the bottom of
               that row it keeps its own height while the picture absorbs whatever the drag
               trades, so the splitter still moves exactly two things. -->
          <Grid Grid.Column="0" Name="MediaColumn" Margin="0,0,14,0">
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
              <!-- Star and star, so the recording and the reading open at half the column each —
                   the even split this tab took later on 2026-08-23 — and the splitter trades one
                   for the other in the same units. Swapped for Auto the moment there is no
                   picture — see ShowPictureRow in MainWindow.axaml.cs, which is also why this is
                   not a binding: a RowDefinition is an AvaloniaObject rather than a StyledElement,
                   so it has no DataContext to bind against. The first row's minimum covers the
                   transport as well as the picture's floor, because both live in it now. -->
              <RowDefinition Height="*" MinHeight="210" />
              <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
              <RowDefinition Height="*" MinHeight="140" />
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <!-- ── The picture, and the controls that move it ─────────────────────────────
                 One row for both. The pane exists only when the open recording has one and the
                 build can draw one — a video pane over an audio file is dead glass, so it exists
                 exactly when frames do — and without one the row is set to Auto and this panel
                 measures to the transport alone, which keeps the controls at the top of the
                 column for a podcast.

                 The Image's bitmap is filled from the code-behind rather than bound: frames
                 arrive at the decoder's rate on the decoder's thread, and thirty property
                 notifications a second is not what bindings are for. See BlitFrame in
                 MainWindow.axaml.cs.

                 Black, and a literal rather than a token: this is the one surface in the window
                 whose ground is the convention of every video player rather than this design's —
                 letterbox bars in Sunken grey would read as a layout bug, not a choice.

                 No height of its own: the row owns it, or a drag would move the row while the
                 pane stayed put and the handle would visibly do nothing. -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Row="0">

              <!-- The transport, flush under the picture. It belongs to the recording rather
                   than to the reading, so it lives on the picture's side of the splitter and is
                   never resized: the pane above it absorbs the whole of a drag. -->
              <Border DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Name="Transport" Classes="tinted taro"
                      Padding="13,11">
                <StackPanel Spacing="8">
                  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,13,*,Auto">

                    <!-- One of the shapes that is genuinely a circle. Two glyphs rather than one
                         that changes: a filled triangle and two bars, each visible on its own half
                         of the state, so neither is drawn by a converter nobody can test. -->
                    <Button Grid.Column="0" Name="PlayPause" Classes="transport"
                            Command="{Binding PlayPauseCommand}"
                            ToolTip.Tip="{Binding PlayPauseLabel}">
                      <Panel Width="13" Height="13">
                        <!-- Nudged right by two: a triangle centred on its bounding box reads as
                             sitting left of centre inside a circle. The corners come from the
                             stroke — Button.transport Path in Theme/Controls.axaml runs each
                             glyph's own colour along its edge with round joins, because a filled
                             polygon alone has needle corners that anti-alias to fuzz at this
                             size. -->
                        <Path Data="M0,0 L11,6.5 L0,13 Z" Stretch="None" Margin="2,0,0,0"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              IsVisible="{Binding !IsPlaying}" />
                        <Path Data="M0,0 H3 V13 H0 Z M8,0 H11 V13 H8 Z" Stretch="None"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              IsVisible="{Binding IsPlaying}" />
                      </Panel>
                    </Button>

                    <!-- The bar, and a transparent strip over it that takes the pointer. The strip
                         is what makes the bar seekable: it knows its own width, so a press at an x
                         is a fraction of the recording, which is a question no binding can answer.
                         18px tall over a 6px bar, so the target is a comfortable one. -->
                    <Panel Grid.Column="2" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                      <ProgressBar Classes="seek" Minimum="0"
                                   Maximum="{Binding DurationSeconds}"
                                   Value="{Binding PositionSeconds}" />

                      <!-- The handle the design asks for, and the one the bar shipped without. The
                           comment on ProgressBar.seek used to say it could not be positioned without
                           a measured width, which was true of a binding and never true of the strip
                           below: that control already knows how wide it is, and already turns an x
                           into a fraction of the recording. So the same arithmetic runs backwards —
                           see PlacePuck in MainWindow.axaml.cs — and the handle is placed from the
                           width the strip was actually given rather than from one nobody measured.

                           Left-aligned with a left margin rather than centred with an offset, so its
                           travel is the strip's width less its own: at the end of a recording it sits
                           inside the bar rather than half past it. -->
                      <Border Name="SeekPuck" Classes="puck" IsVisible="{Binding CanPlay}" />

                      <Border Name="SeekStrip" Background="Transparent" Height="18" Cursor="Hand" />
                    </Panel>

                    <StackPanel Grid.Column="3" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5"
                                VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="13,0,0,0">
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding PositionLabel}" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                 Foreground="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" />
                      <TextBlock Text="/" Classes="meta" />
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding DurationLabel}" Classes="meta" />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Grid>

                  <TextBlock Text="{Binding SelectedRecording.DisplayName}" Classes="meta"
                             TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                             IsVisible="{Binding SelectedRecording, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                  <!-- Why the transport is dead, when it is. A file this build cannot sound is still
                       a file whose transcript is worth reading, so the tab carries on around it. -->
                  <TextBlock Name="PlaybackNotice" Text="{Binding PlaybackNotice}"
                             Classes="fine warning"
                             IsVisible="{Binding PlaybackNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

              <Border Name="VideoPane"
                      Background="Black" IsVisible="{Binding HasVideo}">
                <Image Name="VideoSurface" Stretch="Uniform" />
              </Border>
            </DockPanel>

            <!-- ── The edge that moves ───────────────────────────────────────
                 Drag it and the picture grows or shrinks; the transcript takes back whatever the
                 picture gives up. Direction and behaviour are both named rather than left to
                 resolve: Auto would land on the right answer here by accident, from this strip
                 being wider than it is tall, and a layout that is correct by fallback is one edit
                 away from being wrong.

                 Gone for an audio recording, along with the row it would move — a handle that
                 does nothing is worse than no handle. Its own row stays declared and measures
                 zero around a collapsed child, so no row index shifts.

                 It has to stay a direct child of this Grid: a splitter reads its own Grid.Row off
                 itself, so one nested a panel deeper would read row 0 and move the wrong pair. -->
            <GridSplitter Grid.Row="1" Name="MediaSplitter" Classes="handle"
                          ResizeDirection="Rows" ResizeBehavior="PreviousAndNext"
                          IsVisible="{Binding HasVideo}"
                          ToolTip.Tip="Drag to resize the picture" />

            <!-- ── The reading ────────────────────────────────────────────
                 Everything that is not the recording, in one row, so the drag above can reach
                 none of it but the space it all shares. A DockPanel, because what the order
                 expresses here is a stack rather than a set of sizes. The top margin is the gap
                 under the transport, said here because the transport itself sits flush against
                 the picture on its other side.

                 The transcript is the last child on purpose. LastChildFill is on and that Border
                 carries no Dock, so it fills because it is last; declare anything after it and it
                 collapses to a strip while the newcomer takes the column. -->
            <DockPanel Grid.Row="2" Margin="0,11,0,0">

              <!-- Why there is no picture, when one might be expected and the build cannot draw
                   one. Absent entirely for audio files: no picture is not a limitation there.

                   In this row rather than in the picture's, because it stands in for the picture
                   and is drawn exactly when the picture cannot be: VideoNotice is non-null only
                   where the player cannot draw video at all, which is where HasVideo is false and
                   the pane above has already collapsed. So it reads directly under the transport,
                   in the place the picture would have been explained from. -->
              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Name="VideoNotice" Classes="fine warning"
                         Margin="0,0,0,11"
                         Text="{Binding VideoNotice}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding VideoNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <!-- ── Who is speaking ────────────────────────────────────────
                   The diariser numbers voices; it cannot name them, and it never will — a name is
                   a fact about the world rather than about the audio. So the reader supplies it,
                   and the chips through the whole transcript follow, in both this tab and the
                   Transcribe tab's panes, because every cue of one speaker points at the one
                   object this strip edits.

                   Fields rather than a chip that turns into a field when clicked. The second is
                   the more elegant interaction and the worse one here: nothing would say the names
                   could be changed at all, and the whole difficulty with this feature is that
                   nobody thinks to look for it. Four boxes that obviously take typing say it
                   without a word of instruction.

                   Matcha, inside a tab that is otherwise entirely taro. That looks like a mistake
                   and is the written rule: speaker labelling is a v1 feature, and taro would make
                   it read as a v2 one. See Border.speaker in Theme/Controls.axaml.

                   Absent entirely on a transcript nobody labelled, which is most of them: the
                   labelling pass is opt-in. -->
              <ItemsControl DockPanel.Dock="Top" Name="Voices" ItemsSource="{Binding Speakers}"
                            IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeakers}" Margin="0,0,0,11">
                <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                  <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="7" LineSpacing="7" />
                  </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:SpeakerViewModel">
                    <Border Classes="speaker"
                            Classes.chip0="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}"
                            Classes.chip1="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}"
                            Classes.chip2="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}"
                            Classes.chip3="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}"
                            Classes.chip4="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=4}"
                            Classes.chip5="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=5}"
                            Classes.chip6="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=6}"
                            Classes.chip7="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=7}"
                            ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Label}">
                      <!-- LostFocus, so the transcript is not redrawn on every keystroke of a name
                           being typed. Enter commits by moving focus off the box — see WireVoices
                           in MainWindow.axaml.cs — because a field that only commits when you click
                           somewhere else is a field people think did not work. -->
                      <TextBox Text="{Binding Name, Mode=TwoWay, UpdateSourceTrigger=LostFocus}"
                               PlaceholderText="{Binding Label}"
                               MinWidth="72" />
                    </Border>
                  </DataTemplate>
                </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              </ItemsControl>

              <!-- What a name here is, and is not, said only once one has been given. -->
              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Name="RenameNotice" Classes="fine meta"
                         Margin="0,0,0,11" Text="{Binding RenameNotice}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding RenameNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <!-- ── Transcript or English ───────────────────────────────────
                   The same pill switcher the Transcribe tab has, for the same reason and drawn the
                   same way — but it earns its place twice over here, because the two panes are not
                   equivalent on this tab. The transcript marks the word being said; the English
                   cannot, and never will, because translating loses which word was said when. So
                   this is not "the same thing in another language": it is a choice between reading
                   along with the voice and reading what was meant.

                   Absent for a recording nobody translated, which is most of them.

                   The tab shows the English first when a translation arrives, because asking for
                   one and then having to go and find it is the wrong way round — and it snaps only
                   once, so a reader who comes back to the transcript stays there. -->
              <Border DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="switcher" Name="AskPaneSwitcher"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,0,0,11"
                      IsVisible="{Binding CanShowTranslation}">
                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal">
                  <RadioButton Name="AskPaneTranscript" Classes="segment" GroupName="AskPane"
                               Content="Transcript"
                               IsChecked="{Binding TranscriptPane, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}" />
                  <RadioButton Name="AskPaneEnglish" Classes="segment" GroupName="AskPane"
                               Content="English"
                               IsChecked="{Binding TranscriptPane, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

              <!-- Why no word lights up on this pane. Said rather than left to be noticed: a mark
                   that works on one pane and not the other is indistinguishable from a broken one
                   until somebody explains which. -->
              <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Name="TranslationPaneNotice" Classes="fine meta"
                         Margin="0,0,0,11"
                         Text="{Binding TranslationPaneNotice}"
                         IsVisible="{Binding TranslationPaneNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

              <!-- ── Find a word ────────────────────────────────────────────
                   The reason anyone opens a three-hour transcript is usually one word. This runs
                   on v1's own data and needs no model, which is why it is here rather than waiting
                   for the panel on the right: it is useful before anything can be asked.

                   It does not seek. Finding a word and hearing it are two intentions — somebody
                   scanning for every mention of a name does not want the audio jumping under them
                   on each press of Enter — so a hit is scrolled to and marked, and clicking it is
                   what plays it.

                   At the foot of the words rather than above them, so the column reads top to
                   bottom as the recording, the controls that move it, its words, and the box that
                   searches them. Docked Bottom but still declared before the transcript, because
                   the transcript has to stay the last child. -->
              <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="0,11,0,0"
                    IsEnabled="{Binding HasTranscript}">
                <TextBox Grid.Column="0" Name="SearchBox" Text="{Binding SearchTerm}"
                         PlaceholderText="Find a word: Enter for the next one" />
                <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="7"
                            Margin="9,0,0,0" VerticalAlignment="Center">
                  <TextBlock Name="SearchSummary" Text="{Binding SearchSummary}" Classes="meta"
                             MinWidth="62" TextAlignment="Right" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                  <Button Name="PreviousMatch" Content="Previous" ToolTip.Tip="The hit before this one"
                          Command="{Binding PreviousMatchCommand}" />
                  <Button Name="NextMatch" Content="Next" ToolTip.Tip="The next hit - or press Enter"
                          Command="{Binding NextMatchCommand}" />
                </StackPanel>
              </Grid>

              <Border Classes="panel">
                <Panel>
                  <ScrollViewer Name="CueScroll" IsVisible="{Binding HasTranscript}" Padding="7,9">
                    <ItemsControl Name="Cues" ItemsSource="{Binding Lines}">
                      <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                        <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:TranscriptLineViewModel">
                          <!-- A Button, so the keyboard reaches every cue and the seek is a command
                               like everything else in this window. Its template draws no chrome —
                               see Button.cue — so what a reader sees is a paragraph that lights up
                               under the pointer and stays lit while it is the one being played. -->
                          <Button Classes="cue" Classes.playing="{Binding IsActive}"
                                  Classes.found="{Binding IsCurrentMatch}"
                                  Command="{Binding $parent[ItemsControl].((vm:AskViewModel)DataContext).SeekToLineCommand}"
                                  CommandParameter="{Binding}">
                            <!-- A cue is as tall as its words, which is not what a Button is by
                                 default in this window — see Button.cue in Theme/Controls.axaml for
                                 the height that had to be given back, and for the two defects that
                                 were the same defect. -->
                            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="54,Auto,*">
                              <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="{Binding Timestamp}"
                                         FontSize="11.5" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                                         VerticalAlignment="Top" Margin="0,2,0,0"
                                         Foreground="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" />
                              <Border Grid.Column="1" Margin="0,0,9,0"
                                      IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeaker}"
                                      Classes="speaker"
                                      Classes.chip0="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=0}"
                                      Classes.chip1="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=1}"
                                      Classes.chip2="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=2}"
                                      Classes.chip3="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=3}"
                                      Classes.chip4="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=4}"
                                      Classes.chip5="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=5}"
                                      Classes.chip6="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=6}"
                                      Classes.chip7="{Binding Chip, Converter={StaticResource IndexMatch}, ConverterParameter=7}">
                                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Voice.Name}" />
                              </Border>
                              <!-- Inlines rather than Text, which is what lets one word inside a
                                   paragraph carry a ground of its own — the term that was searched
                                   for, and the word being said right now. Bound to Marked, which is
                                   all three as one value, because a converter takes one thing and
                                   because only the line it happened to raises a change for it: a
                                   keystroke redraws the handful that matched, and a word advancing
                                   redraws the one line being played, rather than the transcript. -->
                              <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Name="CueWords"
                                         Inlines="{Binding Marked, Converter={StaticResource Highlight}}"
                                         TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="20.3" />
                            </Grid>
                          </Button>
                        </DataTemplate>
                      </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl>
                  </ScrollViewer>

                  <TextBlock Name="TranscriptNotice" Text="{Binding TranscriptNotice}"
                             IsVisible="{Binding !HasTranscript}"
                             Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="380"
                             LineHeight="20" Margin="26"
                             HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                             TextAlignment="Center" />
                </Panel>
              </Border>
            </DockPanel>
          </Grid>

          <!-- ── The chat panel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
               Live since 2026-08-24, on the seam built for it: questions go to AskChatViewModel,
               which retrieves evidence windows, streams the model's answer, parses and validates
               it, and hands this layout bullets whose citation chips carry times the application
               resolved — the model's own output never becomes a number on this screen.

               The cover is the same surface that said "work in progress" for two days, kept for
               the states where the panel still cannot work — no engine in the build, no model
               file, no transcript — because a covered control that does not explain itself reads
               as broken. -->
          <Border Grid.Column="1" Classes="panel">
            <Panel>
              <DockPanel Name="AskPanel" IsEnabled="{Binding Chat.IsPanelEnabled}" Margin="15,14">
                <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Text="ASK THIS TRANSCRIPT" Classes="section" />

                <!-- The input, and the one line that explains what just happened to the models. -->
                <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Bottom" Spacing="7" Margin="0,12,0,0">
                  <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             Text="{Binding Chat.ResidencyNotice}"
                             IsVisible="{Binding Chat.ResidencyNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,8,Auto">
                    <TextBox Grid.Column="0" Name="AskInput"
                             Text="{Binding Chat.QuestionText}"
                             PlaceholderText="Ask about this recording…" />
                    <Button Grid.Column="2" Name="AskSend" Content="Ask"
                            Command="{Binding Chat.AskCommand}"
                            IsVisible="{Binding !Chat.IsAsking}" />
                    <!-- The same spot Ask stood in: while an answer is under way — including the
                         minutes a cold model load can take — this is the way out of it. -->
                    <Button Grid.Column="2" Name="AskStop" Content="Stop"
                            Command="{Binding Chat.StopCommand}"
                            IsVisible="{Binding Chat.IsAsking}" />
                  </Grid>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- The suggestions, until the first question makes room for the conversation. -->
                <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top" Spacing="10" Margin="0,11,0,0"
                            IsVisible="{Binding !Chat.HasEntries}">
                  <!-- The second sentence is the non-goals, where a user will look (R13 in the
                       maintainer's plan): local-only, transient, and the model never attributes
                       speech — three promises about what this panel does not do. -->
                  <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                             Text="Answers arrive as short bullets, each carrying the moment it came from, a timestamp you click to hear it, so every claim is one press from being checked. Everything runs on this computer; the conversation is not saved, so copy an answer to keep it, and the model never says who spoke, speaker names stay on the transcript." />
                  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Suggestions}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="6" LineSpacing="6" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="sys:String">
                        <Button Classes="chip" Content="{Binding}"
                                Command="{Binding $parent[ItemsControl].((vm:AskViewModel)DataContext).Chat.AskSuggestionCommand}"
                                CommandParameter="{Binding}" />
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>
                </StackPanel>

                <!-- The conversation. -->
                <ScrollViewer Name="AskChatScroll" Margin="0,11,0,0"
                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                  <ItemsControl Name="AskEntries" ItemsSource="{Binding Chat.Entries}">
                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:ChatEntryViewModel">
                        <StackPanel Spacing="7" Margin="0,0,0,16">

                          <Border Classes="chip" HorizontalAlignment="Right" MaxWidth="260">
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Question}" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                          </Border>

                          <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" Text="{Binding Status}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding Status, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                          <!-- The model is thinking and no answer text exists yet: the typing
                               dots, because a silent panel over a working model reads as a hang. -->
                          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5" Margin="2,4,0,2"
                                      IsVisible="{Binding IsThinking}"
                                      ToolTip.Tip="Thinking…">
                            <StackPanel.Styles>
                              <Style Selector="Ellipse.thinkingDot">
                                <Setter Property="Width" Value="7" />
                                <Setter Property="Height" Value="7" />
                                <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{DynamicResource Taro400Brush}" />
                                <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                <Style.Animations>
                                  <Animation Duration="0:0:1.2" IterationCount="INFINITE">
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="0%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="25%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="50%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="100%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                  </Animation>
                                </Style.Animations>
                              </Style>
                              <Style Selector="Ellipse.thinkingDot.second">
                                <Style.Animations>
                                  <Animation Duration="0:0:1.2" IterationCount="INFINITE" Delay="0:0:0.2">
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="0%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="25%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="50%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="100%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                  </Animation>
                                </Style.Animations>
                              </Style>
                              <Style Selector="Ellipse.thinkingDot.third">
                                <Style.Animations>
                                  <Animation Duration="0:0:1.2" IterationCount="INFINITE" Delay="0:0:0.4">
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="0%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="25%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="50%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                    <KeyFrame Cue="100%">
                                      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.3" />
                                    </KeyFrame>
                                  </Animation>
                                </Style.Animations>
                              </Style>
                            </StackPanel.Styles>
                            <Ellipse Classes="thinkingDot" />
                            <Ellipse Classes="thinkingDot second" />
                            <Ellipse Classes="thinkingDot third" />
                          </StackPanel>

                          <!-- The stream, raw and visibly provisional; the parse replaces it. -->
                          <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                     Text="{Binding StreamingText}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding StreamingText, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                          <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="{Binding Failure}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding Failure, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                          <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="{Binding AbstainedText}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding Abstained}" />

                          <!-- Only when the router answered a whole-recording question from the
                               matched parts anyway: without it a thin answer reads as the
                               recording being thin. -->
                          <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                     Text="{Binding RoutingNotice}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding RoutingNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                          <!-- The overview's framing sentence, above the claims it frames. It is
                               a claim itself, so it carries the same chips and the same
                               [unverified] marker — the only thing it does not carry is a
                               bullet's topic label. -->
                          <ContentControl Content="{Binding Lead}" IsVisible="{Binding HasLead}"
                                          Margin="0,0,0,9">
                            <ContentControl.ContentTemplate>
                              <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:AnswerBulletViewModel">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                                  <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="{Binding Text}" />
                                  <!-- An opening sentence that cites nothing failed no check —
                                       there was none to fail — so it says that rather than
                                       wearing the claim marker. -->
                                  <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary"
                                             Text="{x:Static vm:ChatEntryViewModel.LeadUncitedNotice}"
                                             IsVisible="{Binding IsUncited}" />
                                  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Citations}"
                                                IsEnabled="{Binding $parent[DockPanel].((vm:AskViewModel)DataContext).CanPlay}">
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                        <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="5" LineSpacing="5" />
                                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:CitationChipViewModel">
                                        <Button Classes="chip" Content="{Binding Display}"
                                                Command="{Binding SeekCommand}"
                                                IsEnabled="{Binding IsResolved}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Detail}" />
                                      </DataTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                  </ItemsControl>
                                </StackPanel>
                              </DataTemplate>
                            </ContentControl.ContentTemplate>
                          </ContentControl>

                          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Bullets}" IsVisible="{Binding HasBullets}">
                            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                              <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:AnswerBulletViewModel">
                                <StackPanel Spacing="3" Margin="0,0,0,9">
                                  <TextBlock FontWeight="SemiBold" Text="{Binding Label}"
                                             IsVisible="{Binding HasLabel}" />
                                  <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Text="{Binding Text}" />
                                  <!-- The same marker the copied text carries: an uncited claim
                                       must not read more confident on screen than in an email. -->
                                  <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary"
                                             Text="{x:Static vm:ChatEntryViewModel.UncitedNotice}"
                                             IsVisible="{Binding IsUncited}" />
                                  <!-- No separate quote line: the quoted words sit inside the
                                       claim above, in quotation marks, where the model wrote
                                       them. Only the caveat is still owed. -->
                                  <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" Text="{Binding QuoteCaveat}"
                                             IsVisible="{Binding QuoteCaveat, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
                                  <!-- Disabled with the transport, exactly as the cue rows are: a
                                       chip that silently does nothing reads as broken. -->
                                  <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Citations}"
                                                IsEnabled="{Binding $parent[DockPanel].((vm:AskViewModel)DataContext).CanPlay}">
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                        <WrapPanel ItemSpacing="5" LineSpacing="5" />
                                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                                    <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                      <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:CitationChipViewModel">
                                        <Button Classes="chip" Content="{Binding Display}"
                                                Command="{Binding SeekCommand}"
                                                IsEnabled="{Binding IsResolved}"
                                                ToolTip.Tip="{Binding Detail}" />
                                      </DataTemplate>
                                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                  </ItemsControl>
                                </StackPanel>
                              </DataTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                          </ItemsControl>

                          <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                     Text="{Binding ModelLine}"
                                     IsVisible="{Binding ModelLine, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />

                          <Expander Header="Sources" IsVisible="{Binding HasSources}">
                            <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Sources}"
                                          IsEnabled="{Binding $parent[DockPanel].((vm:AskViewModel)DataContext).CanPlay}">
                              <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:SourceRowViewModel">
                                  <Button Classes="chip" Margin="0,0,0,4"
                                          Command="{Binding SeekCommand}"
                                          HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"
                                          HorizontalContentAlignment="Left">
                                    <StackPanel Spacing="2">
                                      <TextBlock Classes="fine" Text="{Binding TimeRange}" />
                                      <TextBlock Classes="fine secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                                 Text="{Binding Snippet}" />
                                    </StackPanel>
                                  </Button>
                                </DataTemplate>
                              </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                            </ItemsControl>
                          </Expander>

                          <Button Content="Copy answer" Command="{Binding CopyCommand}"
                                  IsVisible="{Binding CanCopy}" HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
                        </StackPanel>
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
                  </ItemsControl>
                </ScrollViewer>
              </DockPanel>

              <!-- The cover, only while the panel cannot work, saying which of its three
                   prerequisites is missing. The panel's own ground at 82%, so what is underneath
                   reads as waiting rather than switched off. -->
              <Border Name="AskNotice" Classes="wip"
                      IsVisible="{Binding Chat.PanelNotice, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Chat.PanelNotice}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           MaxWidth="250" Margin="24"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           FontSize="11.5" LineHeight="18.5" TextAlignment="Center"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource Taro700Brush}" />
              </Border>
            </Panel>
          </Border>
        </Grid>
        </DockPanel>

        <!-- ── The recordings drawer ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
             The queue, floating over the page. The same rows the Transcribe tab is filling, not
             a copy of them — everything in the queue, not only what has finished: a file is
             playable the moment it is dropped.

             Three ways out, because a surface that covers the page has to be easy to leave:
             choosing a recording closes it (the choice is the errand it was opened for — see
             OnSelectedRecordingChanged in AskViewModel), the Close button closes it, and the
             scrim underneath closes it on any press outside — which includes the press on the
             toggle the drawer is covering, so the button's two states cannot trap anyone.

             The scrim is transparent rather than a wash: a dimmed page says modal, and nothing
             about reading a list of recordings needs the rest of the window taken away. It is
             hit-testable because it names a Background — a null one is not — and its press is
             wired in WireRecordingsDrawer in MainWindow.axaml.cs, because a plain Border has no
             command to bind. -->
        <Border Name="DrawerScrim" Background="Transparent"
                IsVisible="{Binding IsRecordingsDrawerOpen}" />

        <Border Name="RecordingsDrawer" Width="280"
                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                Background="{DynamicResource GroundBrush}"
                BorderBrush="{DynamicResource Taro200Brush}"
                BorderThickness="1"
                BoxShadow="8 0 26 0 #30000000"
                IsVisible="{Binding IsRecordingsDrawerOpen}">
          <DockPanel Margin="13">
            <Grid DockPanel.Dock="Top" ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" Margin="0,0,0,9">
              <TextBlock Text="RECORDINGS" Classes="section" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              <Button Grid.Column="1" Name="CloseRecordings" Content="Close"
                      Command="{Binding CloseRecordingsDrawerCommand}" />
            </Grid>
            <TextBlock DockPanel.Dock="Top" Classes="fine meta" Margin="0,0,0,9"
                       IsVisible="{Binding !HasRecordings}"
                       Text="Nothing in the queue yet. Drop a file on the Transcribe tab and it appears here, playable straight away." />
            <ListBox ItemsSource="{Binding Recordings}" SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedRecording}">
              <ListBox.ItemTemplate>
                <DataTemplate x:DataType="vm:JobViewModel">
                  <StackPanel Spacing="3">
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding DisplayName}" Classes="emphasis"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="meta" />
                  </StackPanel>
                </DataTemplate>
              </ListBox.ItemTemplate>
            </ListBox>
          </DockPanel>
        </Border>
        </Panel>
      </TabItem>

      <!-- ══ Settings ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           How a run is done rather than what it produces, which is the line between this page and
           Export: a format is a fact about the file that comes out, and everything here is a fact
           about the pass that makes it.

           Index 5, appended, so nothing before it moves. Same reading-page shape as Export.

           Matcha throughout and not one taro token, which is the design's own rule rather than a
           preference: a surface's colour says which product generation it belongs to, and every
           control on this page is v1. See Theme/Tokens.axaml.

           What is deliberately NOT here: the backend picker, which stays on the Models tab beside
           the Load button it governs; the launch update check, which stays on Updates beside the
           sentence explaining it; and the two extra passes, which are on the Transcribe tab
           beside the queue they run over. All are settings; all are more use next to the thing
           they change than in a drawer with everything else. -->
      <TabItem Header="Settings">
        <DockPanel Margin="26,22">

          <StackPanel DockPanel.Dock="Top" Spacing="6" Margin="0,0,0,12" MaxWidth="700"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left">
            <TextBlock Text="Settings" Classes="title" />
            <TextBlock Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640"
                       HorizontalAlignment="Left" LineHeight="20"
                       Text="Everyday choices are on General. Advanced holds the knobs that change how the work is actually done, named there by what they really are. Switching speaker labels and translation on is on the Transcribe tab." />
          </StackPanel>

          <!-- Two sub-tabs rather than one long page, added 2026-08-29. The page had grown to
               five sections in which a button anyone might press (update yt-dlp when YouTube
               breaks it) sat between a segment cap and an expert-placement picker. The split is
               by one rule: Advanced is anything that can make the output worse, refuse to load,
               or that needs a measurement to set correctly. Everything else is General.

               Two headings appear on both tabs, and that is the rule applied rather than a
               failure of it: the graphics offer and the access token are things a person needs
               to get speaker labelling working at all, while the device picker and the batch
               size are ways to break it. Splitting the heading is more honest than putting
               either half in the wrong place. -->
          <TabControl Name="SettingsSubTabs">

            <TabItem Header="General">
              <ScrollViewer>
                <StackPanel Spacing="15" MaxWidth="700" HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,14,0,0">

              <StackPanel Spacing="9"
                          DataContext="{Binding $parent[Window].DataContext}"
                          x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel">
              <!-- yt-dlp and Deno. The one place this product fetches something newer than it
                   shipped with, and the reason is that a pinned yt-dlp has a shelf life of weeks:
                   YouTube changes what it serves and yt-dlp changes to match. Everything else here
                   is pinned and verified against a hash committed in the repository; this is
                   verified against the hash the publisher serves beside the download, which is
                   weaker and is the trade being made. See ToolUpdater.

                   No IsVisible: unlike the CUDA block this applies to every machine, and a build
                   that vendored neither tool says so in the line under the button rather than
                   hiding a control whose absence would be its own puzzle. -->
              <TextBlock Text="DOWNLOADING FROM LINKS" Classes="section" />

              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding ToolsExplanation}" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <Button Content="Update yt-dlp and Deno"
                        Command="{Binding UpdateToolsCommand}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ToolVersions}" Classes="meta" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>

              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         IsVisible="{Binding HasToolsMessage}"
                         Text="{Binding ToolsMessage}" />

              <TextBlock Text="SPEAKER LABELS" Classes="section" Margin="0,8,0,0" />

              <!-- The CUDA pack. Drawn only on a machine whose driver has CUDA, or one that already
                   has the pack — a computer that cannot use this is not shown a 1.8 GB download and
                   is not told about one either, because a disabled row with an explanation is an
                   advertisement for something that will never apply. `CudaDriverProbe` decides, and
                   it asks nvcuda.dll rather than an adapter name; its third answer, "could not
                   tell", counts as no.

                   The button is separately disabled while the manifest is unverified, which is the
                   state this build ships in: the digests describe a local build and no release
                   asset has been uploaded, so there is nothing to download yet. The explanation
                   says so in words rather than leaving a dead button unexplained. -->
              <StackPanel Spacing="6" IsVisible="{Binding ShowCudaPack}">
                <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           Text="{Binding CudaPackExplanation}" />

                <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8"
                            IsVisible="{Binding CanOfferCudaPack}">
                  <Button Content="Set up graphics acceleration"
                          Command="{Binding InstallCudaPackCommand}" />
                  <ProgressBar Width="160" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Minimum="0" Maximum="1"
                               Value="{Binding CudaPackProgress}"
                               IsVisible="{Binding IsInstallingCudaPack}" />
                </StackPanel>

                <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasCudaPackMessage}"
                           Text="{Binding CudaPackMessage}" />
              </StackPanel>
              <!-- The access token for the speaker model whose download is gated. Here rather than
                   on the Models tab because this is where the settings file is written, and beside
                   the other speaker settings because it belongs to one of those models.

                   PasswordChar so it is not left on screen or in a screenshot; RevealPassword is
                   deliberately not offered, because the value can always be pasted again and a
                   reveal toggle is one more place for it to be shown by accident. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Hugging Face token" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBox Text="{Binding HuggingFaceToken}" MinWidth="260" PasswordChar="•"
                         PlaceholderText="hf_…" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="One of the speaker models is only handed out to people who have accepted its terms. Open its page from the Models tab, accept them, then create a read-only access token on your Hugging Face account and paste it here. It is stored in your settings file as you typed it, and is sent to huggingface.co and nowhere else. Leave this empty if you are not using that model." />

              <TextBlock Text="ASKING" Classes="section" Margin="0,8,0,0" />
              <DockPanel LastChildFill="True">
                <ToggleSwitch DockPanel.Dock="Left" Margin="0,0,9,0"
                              Content="Think before answering"
                              IsChecked="{Binding AskThinking}" />
                <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           Text="The model works through the question privately before it answers. Answers are often more thorough and take noticeably longer. Takes effect at your next question." />
              </DockPanel>
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Model" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding AskModels}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedAskModel}" MinWidth="320" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding AskModelExplanation}" />

            </StackPanel>

            <!-- ── About ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
                 Where the Licences tab went on 2026-08-23. It was the fifth page of a five-page
                 application and the one page nobody opens twice; a tab strip is for places you go,
                 and a notice package is a thing you look up. It is here rather than anywhere else
                 because a Settings page is where every desktop application in the world puts it,
                 and being findable is the entire job of this button. -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="9">
              <TextBlock Text="ABOUT" Classes="section" />
              <TextBlock Classes="secondary" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Left" LineHeight="20"
                         Text="Which build this is, the licences and notices that come with the models and the components it is built from, and the machine facts a bug report asks for." />
              <Button Name="ShowAbout" Classes="onPage" Content="About Uindosill"
                      HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
            </StackPanel>

                </StackPanel>
              </ScrollViewer>
            </TabItem>

            <TabItem Header="Advanced">
              <ScrollViewer>
                <StackPanel Spacing="15" MaxWidth="700" HorizontalAlignment="Left" Margin="0,14,0,0">

                  <!-- The one warning on this page, in the window&apos;s single amber, on the terms
                       the Models tab&apos;s uninstall notice set. Not decoration: every control
                       below can make a transcript worse, and three can stop a model loading. -->
                  <Border Background="{DynamicResource WarningPanelBrush}"
                          BorderBrush="{DynamicResource WarningPanelEdgeBrush}"
                          BorderThickness="1" Padding="12,10">
                    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="9">
                      <Path Stroke="{DynamicResource StatusWarningBrush}" StrokeThickness="1.5"
                            StrokeLineCap="Round" StrokeJoin="Round"
                            Data="M8 2.9l5.6 10.2H2.4z M8 6.6v3.1 M8 11.5v.1"
                            Width="15" Height="15" Stretch="Uniform" VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                      <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" Classes="warning" FontSize="11.5" MaxWidth="560"
                                 Text="Leave these alone unless you know what they do. They change how the audio is cut, which arithmetic unit each model runs on, and how much it holds at once. A wrong choice makes transcripts worse or stops a model loading, and the effect is not always visible in the result. Every default here was measured; none of the alternatives has been." />
                    </StackPanel>
                  </Border>

                  <StackPanel Spacing="15" DataContext="{Binding Transcribe}"
                              x:DataType="vm:TranscribeViewModel">
            <!-- ── How the audio is cut ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
            <StackPanel Spacing="9">
              <TextBlock Text="HOW THE AUDIO IS CUT" Classes="section" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="14">
                <CheckBox Content="Fixed windows instead of speech detection"
                          IsChecked="{Binding UseFixedWindows}" />
                <TextBlock Text="Segment cap" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <NumericUpDown Value="{Binding MaxSegmentSeconds}" Minimum="5" Maximum="60"
                               Increment="5" Width="110" FormatString="0 's'" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- The detection box — ticked by default whenever its model is installed, since
                   later on 2026-08-23 — drawn on the speaker one's terms: a DockPanel so the hint
                   wraps, the box disabled with the reason beside it rather than hidden, and the
                   reason is one a person can act on from the Models tab. Beneath the fixed-windows
                   row because the two are about the same cut, and the hint says so when fixed
                   windows are on. -->
              <DockPanel LastChildFill="True" Name="SpeechDetectionRow">
                <CheckBox DockPanel.Dock="Left"
                          Content="Neural speech detection"
                          Margin="0,0,9,0"
                          VerticalAlignment="Center"
                          IsChecked="{Binding UseNeuralSpeechDetection}"
                          IsEnabled="{Binding CanUseNeuralSpeechDetection}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding SpeechDetectionHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center"
                           IsVisible="{Binding SpeechDetectionHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
              </DockPanel>

              <!-- The ceiling the cap sits under, which is a fact about the recogniser rather than
                   a setting: the cap above chooses a length up to 60 seconds, and the pipeline cuts
                   at 30 whatever it says. It followed the cap here from the foot of the Transcribe
                   tab on 2026-08-23, because an explanation of a control on another page is a
                   paragraph nobody can act on. -->
              <TextBlock Name="SegmentationNote" Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="Every recording is cut into segments of at most 30 seconds before it is decoded, because the recogniser loses accuracy on longer stretches. A cap above 30 has no effect." />
            </StackPanel>

            <!-- There is no spoken-language picker on this page, and that is a decision rather than
                 an omission. parakeet.cpp's ABI does take a target language, but the tdt
                 checkpoints this catalogue ships are not prompt-conditioned and never read it:
                 four runs over one clip — no hint, `en`, `de`, and the nonexistent `zz` — produced
                 byte-identical output at exit 0, where a prompt-conditioned model would have failed
                 the `zz` decode (docs/UNPROVEN.md § The language hint). A control that changes
                 nothing is the failure this window refuses to ship, and one drawn permanently
                 disabled is still a control nobody can use. It comes back when a checkpoint that
                 reads the hint does.

                 It would not have been a translation control either way: the translator behind
                 the Transcribe tab's opt-in is many-to-one, told the target and never the
                 source. -->
              <TextBlock Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="UseFixedWindows, MaxSegmentSeconds and UseNeuralSpeechDetection are the segmentation stage. The detector is Silero VAD through Parakeet.Engine.SileroVad; fixed windows bypass it entirely. These three are not written to settings.json and reset when the window closes." />
                  </StackPanel>

            <Separator />

              <StackPanel Spacing="9"
                          DataContext="{Binding $parent[Window].DataContext}"
                          x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel">
              <TextBlock Text="SPEAKER LABELS" Classes="section" />

              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Run on" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding DiarisationProviders}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedDiarisationProvider}" MinWidth="200" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding DiarisationProviderExplanation}" />
              <TextBlock Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="diarisationProvider takes auto, cpu or cuda, which name a torch device; webgpu and dml name ONNX Runtime execution providers and need graphs this window derives. The CLI spells it --backend on uindosill diarise. A named provider refuses rather than falling back; auto falls through to the cpu." />
              <!-- The graphics option's one-time preparation speaks here: what it is doing while it
                   runs, and what went wrong if it could not. Hidden the rest of the time rather
                   than left as an empty line, because a permanently reserved gap under a setting
                   reads as something missing. -->
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         IsVisible="{Binding HasSpeakerGraphsMessage}"
                         Text="{Binding SpeakerGraphsMessage}" />
              <!-- Disabled with the reason beside it rather than hidden, on the speech-detection
                   row's terms: a control that vanishes when a different model is picked reads as a
                   bug, and the reason is one a person can act on from the Models tab. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Audio held at once" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding DiarisationBatchSizes}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedDiarisationBatchSize}" MinWidth="220"
                          IsEnabled="{Binding CanSetDiarisationBatchSize}" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding DiarisationBatchSizeHint}" Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" MaxWidth="300"
                           IsVisible="{Binding DiarisationBatchSizeHint, Converter={x:Static ObjectConverters.IsNotNull}}" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding DiarisationBatchSizeExplanation}" />
              <TextBlock Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="diarisationBatchSize is pyannote&apos;s segmentation_batch_size and embedding_batch_size, set together. Unset leaves the checkpoint&apos;s own value. A memory setting, not a speed one." />
            </StackPanel>

            <Separator />

              <StackPanel Spacing="9"
                          DataContext="{Binding $parent[Window].DataContext}"
                          x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel">
              <TextBlock Text="ASKING" Classes="section" />
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Answer from" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding AskModes}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedAskMode}" MinWidth="200" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding AskModeExplanation}" />

              <TextBlock Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="askMode is the router. Automatic lets each question choose between the transcript and the model&apos;s own knowledge." />
              <!-- How much evidence an answer is built from. A setting rather than a new default
                   because only half of the trade has been measured: the time it saves is large and
                   repeatable, and what it costs in finding the right part of the recording is not
                   yet known. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Searching" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding AskEvidenceLevels}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedAskEvidence}" MinWidth="240" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="Answers are built from the parts of the recording that best match your question. Searching more of it takes longer (on a laptop, roughly twice as long), and searching less may miss the part that held the answer." />

              <TextBlock Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="askEvidence is how much of the transcript is searched per question. Only half of this trade is measured: the time saved is, what it costs in finding the right passage is not." />
              <!-- The expert placement. Here rather than left as a constant because the constant
                   was the second machine's answer applied to every machine: integrated graphics
                   need the experts in system memory or a large mixture will not load, and a card
                   with memory of its own should be holding them. Automatic asks the loader; the
                   two fixed rows are for a machine it reads wrongly. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
                <TextBlock Text="Expert layers" Classes="secondary" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <ComboBox ItemsSource="{Binding AskExpertPlacements}"
                          SelectedItem="{Binding SelectedAskExpertPlacement}" MinWidth="200" />
              </StackPanel>
              <TextBlock Classes="meta" TextWrapping="Wrap" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="{Binding AskExpertPlacementExplanation}" />
              <TextBlock Classes="fine meta" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                         Text="askExpertPlacement sets --cpu-moe on the llama-server child, deciding whether a mixture-of-experts model&apos;s expert tensors are held in system memory instead of on the card. The 26B-A4B is 85% experts, so this moves 13.4 of its 15.8 GiB. Automatic uses a fit rule on Vulkan only; on CUDA it leaves placement to the loader, which was measured holding that model at 22.4 tok/s with nothing offloaded. Both explicit choices apply on CUDA and Vulkan; none applies on the CPU backend, where the weights are in system memory already." />
            </StackPanel>

                </StackPanel>
              </ScrollViewer>
            </TabItem>

          </TabControl>
        </DockPanel>
      </TabItem>

    </TabControl>
  </DockPanel>
  </Border>
</Window>
